// ═══ ABSTRACT CLASS: cannot instantiate directly ═══
public abstract class Account {
    private final String accountId;
    protected BigDecimal balance;

    protected Account(String accountId, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public abstract BigDecimal calculateInterest();  // abstract — no body
    public abstract String getType();                // abstract — no body

    public String getAccountId() { return accountId; }
    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
}

// (Avoid) COMPILE ERROR:
// Account account = new Account("SAV001", BigDecimal.ZERO);
// "Cannot instantiate the type Account" — it's abstract!

// (Recommended) NAMED SUBCLASS (separate class file):
public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    public SavingsAccount(String id, BigDecimal bal) { super(id, bal); }
    @Override public BigDecimal calculateInterest() { return balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.04")); }
    @Override public String getType() { return "SAVINGS"; }
}
Account named = new SavingsAccount("SAV001", new BigDecimal("125000"));

// (Recommended) ANONYMOUS SUBCLASS (inline — no separate class file!):
Account anonymous = new Account("ANON001", new BigDecimal("50000")) {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest() {
        return getBalance().multiply(new BigDecimal("0.03"));  // 3% — custom rate
    }

    @Override
    public String getType() {
        return "ANONYMOUS";
    }
};
// This creates: an unnamed class extending Account, implementing both abstract methods.
// anonymous.calculateInterest() → 1500
// anonymous.getType() → "ANONYMOUS"
// anonymous.getClass().getSimpleName() → "" (empty — truly anonymous!)
// anonymous.getClass().getName() → "com.bank.Main$1" (compiler-generated name)
```

Anonymous class vs Lambda:

```java
// ═══ BEFORE Java 8: anonymous class for Comparator ═══
Comparator<Trade> oldWay = new Comparator<Trade>() {
    @Override
    public int compare(Trade a, Trade b) {
        return a.getAmount().compareTo(b.getAmount());
    }
};

// ═══ AFTER Java 8: lambda (only for FUNCTIONAL INTERFACE — one abstract method) ═══
Comparator<Trade> newWay = (a, b) -> a.getAmount().compareTo(b.getAmount());
// Shorter! But ONLY works for interfaces with ONE abstract method.

// ═══ ABSTRACT CLASS: lambda DOESN'T work! Must use anonymous class. ═══
// Account lambda = (id, bal) -> { ... };  // (Avoid) COMPILE ERROR! Account is not a functional interface!
// Lambda works ONLY for interfaces, not abstract classes.
// Must use anonymous subclass for abstract classes.
```

When anonymous subclass is still useful (even with lambdas):

```java
// 1. ABSTRACT CLASS — lambda doesn't work:
Account testAccount = new Account("TEST", BigDecimal.ZERO) {
    @Override public BigDecimal calculateInterest() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
    @Override public String getType() { return "TEST"; }
};
// Useful in tests — quick one-off implementation without creating a full class file.

// 2. INTERFACE with MULTIPLE abstract methods — lambda doesn't work:
interface TradeValidator {
    boolean validateAmount(BigDecimal amount);
    boolean validateSymbol(String symbol);
}
TradeValidator validator = new TradeValidator() {
    @Override public boolean validateAmount(BigDecimal amount) { return amount.signum() > 0; }
    @Override public boolean validateSymbol(String symbol) { return symbol != null; }
};
// Two abstract methods → not a functional interface → lambda impossible.

// 3. NEED TO OVERRIDE MULTIPLE METHODS or add state:
Comparator<Trade> withLogging = new Comparator<Trade>() {
    private int compareCount = 0;  // STATE in anonymous class!
    @Override public int compare(Trade a, Trade b) {
        compareCount++;
        return a.getAmount().compareTo(b.getAmount());
    }
};
// Lambda can't have instance fields. Anonymous class can.
```

